::ALG2_U02_L01_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the degree of f(x) = 3x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 9? {
=2 #Correct. The highest exponent is 2.
~3 #This is the leading coefficient.
~4 #There is no fourth power.
~9 #This is the constant term.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which form best shows the zeros of f(x) = (x - 5)(x + 2)? {
~standard form #Standard form does not show factors directly.
~vertex form #Vertex form shows the vertex.
=factored form #Correct. Factored form shows zeros.
~table form #A table may suggest zeros but not structure.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What are the zeros of f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> - 7x + 12? {
~x = -3 and x = -4 #The signs are reversed.
=x = 3 and x = 4 #Correct. It factors as (x - 3)(x - 4).
~x = -7 and x = 12 #These are coefficients.
~x = 0 and x = 12 #The constant is not automatically a zero.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the y-intercept of f(x) = 2x<sup>2</sup> - 6x + 5? {
~2 #This is the leading coefficient.
~-6 #This is the linear coefficient.
~0 #f(0) is not 0.
=5 #Correct. f(0) = 5.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the vertex of g(x) = (x - 3)<sup>2</sup> + 8? {
~(-3, 8) #The sign is reversed.
=(3, 8) #Correct. Vertex is (3, 8).
~(8, 3) #Coordinates are reversed.
~(3, -8) #The k-value is positive.
}

